From b186ee1c4b5bbfa45adc2a2cb9c11cc097091361
Subject: Web: cancel a Task when its handle is dropped
Origin: https://github.com/robtfm/bevy/commit/b186ee1c4b5bbfa45adc2a2cb9c11cc097091361
Date: 2026-10-30T12:40:11Z

On the web `Task` was a oneshot receiver over a `spawn_local` driver, so
dropping the handle left the future running to completion; natively the
drop cancels. Code that relied on "drop the handle, the work stops" was
right natively and silently wrong on the web, and had to cancel case by
case (bevy_asset's path_to_id poll, the explorer's imposter token and
shared-download oneshot).

The driver now runs inside an `Abortable`: dropping the handle aborts it
at its next poll and its locals drop with it (a reqwest fetch's
AbortGuard, an ipfs request slot). `detach()` forgets the abort handle,
`cancel()` aborts then awaits the output, and `is_finished()` and native's
`#[must_use]` come along. The relay to the JS thread and the async-worker
path need no change: the abort registration rides inside the driver.

Two debug logs name the output type of a task dropped while pending and
of a driver that was aborted, so cancellations can be traced with
`bevy_tasks=debug`.
